Date: Tue, 4 May 1993 21:29:55 -0600
From: twb3@midway.uchicago.edu (Tom Morrow)
Subject: Meta: Privatizing Judges

>Let me propose a variation:
>Each case is binary, and most cases are between list members.  But the
>list administrator (LA) must be informed of all cases, and may bring
>suit on behalf of the list itself on a related issue.  The LA could be
>both prosecutor and judge in such cases, or we might perhaps seperate
>these roles.
>
>Robin Hanson

I'm not sure what effect your proposal has on present practices, Robin,
except to provide that the List Administrator be informed of private
adjudication.  And I'm not sure why this is necessary.  Is it that the need
for private adjudication signals potential violation of public peace? 
Maybe, but this rule discourages private adjudication.  Not that that has
ever been very popular . . .

The problem has never been access to adjudication.  Those caught in flame
wars have always had the option of picking a third party to settle their
disputes.  If they choose an abitrator and take their fight off-list, then
the rest of us could care less what happens.  The problem is that usually
at least one of the flamers likes to keep the dispute public.  I think that
then the List Administrator thus has the right to force a settlement of the
dispute, or at least to ensure that it does not continue in public.

Sometimes one of the parties will complain to the LA.  Then the LA should
use his authority to force both parties to either choose an adjudicator or
use his services.  If the parties opt for the former choice, then I don't
see why the rest of us should care about the outcome of this private
dispute.  The fight is off the list, and that is what we want.  As Robin
notes, though, the LA may bring separate charges for disrupting the peace.

If the disputants choose the LA, the LA will apply the list's customary and
codified law to achieve a result consonant with list policies.  End of
story.

If neither party complains, but third parties go to the LA and ask that the
flame war be stopped, The LA can step in and order the parties to seek some
sort of adjudication.  Again, the LA may append charges designed to protect
the public weal, to be tried separately from a privately settled dispute or
as part of one held entirely in public.

Actually, I don't think Robin and I disagree much about this.  I only doubt
that the LA need be informed about private adjudication.  Apart from that,
we appear to agree that the LA should give disputants the right to seek
private adjudication, while retaining the right to bring separate charges
for infringement of the public interest.

