Date: Tue, 04 May 93 21:24:22 +0100
From: Eric Watt Forste <arkuat@joes.garage.com>
Subject: free banking

Newbie post alert: I'm probably being overenthusiastic in the
following, or something, but please pardon my exuberance.

THE THEORY OF FREE BANKING by George Selgin
 
A call for action and book review
 
Those of you who caught my post on "meme warfare" know that my
favorite Extropian (tm) issue is the replacement of the state with
systems based on spontaneous order, and know why. Digital cash is the
most important tool we have to finish off the state in the coming
decades, because only the protection of encryption, embodied in a
mature and thriving free digicash banking industry, can give the
underground economy the protection from the state it needs to thrive
and flourish. As the underground economy expands to encompass and
protect more and more of the productive activities from which the
state derives its sustenance, the state will shrink for lack of funds.
 
This happy circumstance can only happen with the establishment of free
banking. Whether free banks will use gold or tobacco or S&P 500
contracts or even dollar-denominated FRNs as their reserve currency is
not of great importance, as different banks will use different reserve
currencies responding to the demands of the market (just as banks
today compete internationally in offering accounts based in dollars,
marks, francs, etc.). What is important is that such banks are
established, protected from the state, and made accessible to those
who wish to use them.
 
Most of the readers on this list are familiar with the encryption
technology that will enable such banks. Yet it seems that relatively
few are familiar with the economics of free banking. Free banking has
not existed in any major nation for most of this century, has never
existed in the United States (with the exception of a brief period in
some New England states), and has been practiced in most developed
countries at no time in history. It is not surprising, then, that so
few of us understand how banking without a Central Banking Authority
can function. Yet the people who actually establish and operate the
first fully-private digicash banks will require an understanding of
the theory of free banking as fully as they will require an
understanding of public-key encryption technology.
 
To this end, I suggest that THE THEORY OF FREE BANKING (TFB) by George
Selgin might be added to the various Extropian book lists. TFB was
written primarily for the benefit of upper-division and graduate
economics students, yet the economic principles involved in its
discussion are simple enough for me (I am neither an economics student
nor a college graduate). You can pick up enough economics to
understand this text just from a close reading of THE MACHINERY OF
FREEDOM by David Friedman, although PRICE THEORY, an excellent and
very readable textbook by the same author, would put you in even
better stead.
 
TFB not only builds up a good theoretical analysis of the operation of
free banks and their issuance of fiduciary media, but also succinctly
criticizes the failures of central banking and the causes of these
failures. The most elaborate chapters of the book analyze how free
banks would respond to changes in the demand for money, because change
in the demand for money is the primary force generating price
inflation or deflation under conditions of fixed money supply. TFB
shows how prices would probably be more stable under a system allowing
free competitive issuance of fiduciary media than under a system of
fixed money supply. TFB also shows how the supply of fiduciary media
can adjust to changes in the supply of reserve currency (a big gold
strike, for instance, in a gold-based system) in a way that stabilizes
prices, thus demonstrating that *what* the reserve currency is is not
as important as a free market in banking services, including the
issuance of banknotes.
 
There is lots of good stuff in this book that you will find neither in
Ludwig von Mises nor in Milton Friedman (at least not his earlier
stuff). It's a very fast read for an econ text. I cannot recommend it
highly enough, particularly to anyone with any interest at all in
actually creating a digicash economy.
 
I personally would like to open my digicash account as soon as
possible, and I'd like it to become very useful to me as soon as
possible. That's why I'm pushing this book.
 
George A. Selgin. 1988. The Theory of Free Banking: Money Supply under
Competitive Note Issue. Totowa, NJ: Rowman & Littlefield.
 
ISBN 0-8476-7578-5
 
