      In the last 12 minutes you have made 137 postings on subjects I think are
      related to yogic UFO reincarnation, totalling 800 words. It seems to me 
      that, whilst I am not yet in a position to criticise your choice of 
      subject, you might consider posting less frequently on the subject, 
      giving yourself time to formulate more cogent arguments, and generally 
      reducing the noise level on the list.') 
   to filing a charge of gratuitously wasting cpu time. No doubt the machine
   would select an appropriately interesting arbitrator for the case; probably
   Richard Depew... The information on the outcome of the case would also be 
   fed back into the `potential lawsuit detector' to further refine 
   discriminatory abilities. Eventually, we might even end up with a genuine 
   flame-war/non-E-topic detector, which could be licensed to other mailing 
   lists to remove their flames too, thus...

2. I suspect at least 6 people will agree with me that 
   list-as-extropian-metalogue is, at the very least, an amusing idea. So, as 
   there are at least 5 people who suspect that someday 
   strong-enough-to-be-useful AI will be developed, and at least almost 
   everyone interested in legal issues, we perhaps might consider allowing the
   computer to bring charges to be an experiment in future interfacing of AI's
   and the legal system. Clearly, at least initially, the computer's attempts
   at finding actionable offences will have to be screened by more experienced
   (read human) observers, but as the system progresses, it can be left more
   and more to its own devices. Of course, if it get sufficiently developed, 
   it too will seek ways of getting round the slow legal system that doesn't 
   produce the verdicts it wants, and probably introduce its own mail 
   filtering, removals of posting privileges, and so forth ;->.

3. While I'm at it, the thought of the reputation of the computer springs to 
   mind. If it proves to be a sufficiently good detector of flames, and so 
   establishes its reputation, then it's quite probable that any 
   reputation-filtering system will have to bear this in mind; but exactly 
   how, I can't forsee at the moment.

Well, that's my 54-lines-worth.

