From: eder@hsvaic.boeing.com (Dani Eder)
Subject: A market mechanism for the extropians list

The problems in the list seem to be caused by a lack of strong
market mechanisms.  We have the same problem on the list as the
health care system - no marginal cost.  It costs us nothing to
post, so we overuse the service.  

I propose creating a scarcity of posting ability, and a market
mechanism to price the commodity.  This is how it would work:

Each person may 'subscribe' to the postings of a list of people.
For example, I, Dani Eder, amy subscribe to the postings of
Tom Morrow and Perry Metzger.  Each member of the extropians
list gets a small posting budget for free (say 2K per week), plus
an increment (say 100 bytes/week) per subscriber to their postings.

You may accumulate posting credits over time to be able to post
big things.  The list software would have to be able to track
subscription/drop requests by author, accumulate allocations,
subtract consumption by postings, and deny posting to people
who are out of budget.

ExI could potentially sell posting credits to raise money, and
list members could potentially barter/buy/sell credits.  
Then if a nusiance like Mr. Singh really wanted to annoy us with
his postings, an no one subscribed to them, he would at least
be subsidizing ExI by buying his postings.  It would be a form
of advertising, and we have the same choice to skip the ads 
on the list as we do in any other commercial publication.

The budget numbers I mentioned above are examples only, but I
think the figure should be set on a slightly stringent basis,
so as to encourage conciseness.

Comments?


