From: eder@hsvaic.boeing.com (Dani Eder)
Subject: A market mechanism for the extropians list -some notes

Mr. Weeks asks how do new list members build subscribers.  The
answer is either (a) they make good, short posts (2K per week
allows a new lurker to post more than 1000 words in their
first post, if they lurk for a month).  If they pick up 20
subscribers, they could double their posting capacity.

Alternatively, they could buy posting credits from someone else
to establish themselves, they could borrow posting credits with
a promise to pay back later with interest, or they could send
email directly to individuals on the list asking for subscriptions
to get started.

On the other side of the coin, if a poster at first sounds
reasonable, but later starts raving about flying yogis or whatever,
the loss of subscribers acts as an automatic correction mechanism
to 'lower the volume' on that person.

A slight modification to the software I would make would be to
set a minimum byte debit for small posts.  For example, any post
under 1000 bytes gets counted as 1000 bytes.  This is intended
to preclude someone being a nusiance by posting 20 100-byte messages
per week.  The mail software being what it is on most computers,
we take a certain amount of time per message, even to throw them
away.  This cost floor per posting will make the cost commensurate
with the time we expend in filtering things out of the mail list.

Again, don't take the specific value of 1000 bytes as graven in
stone, although I do think it is in the right order of magnitude.

Dani Eder

